  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1048 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this evening displays an \nupper-level trough approaching the Great Lakes region with another \njust off the coast of the western CONUS. Between these features, we \nreside in a brief low-amplitude flow pattern across the central and \nsouthern Plains. At the surface, a large corridor of high pressure \ncentered over the northern Plains will spill into the area through \nthe period. Apart from some passing stratus across northeast\nNebraska, radiational cooling tonight will allow low \ntemperatures to fall into the mid teens to low 20s. Weak CAA \nwill continue to filter in, keeping high temperatures in the \nupper 30s and low 40s on Monday. After a cool day on Monday with\nlight northerly winds, cloud cover will filter into the area \nMonday night into Tuesday. Low-level flow will shift to \nsoutherly Tuesday as the surface high pressure pulls to the \neast. However, the southerly flow won't have much of an impact \non temperatures as continued cloud cover confines high \ntemperatures to the upper 30s and low 40s on Tuesday. \n\nWednesday and Beyond...\n\nThe current pattern will be disrupted on Wednesday as a shortwave \ntrough swings southward out of Alberta/Saskatchewan and towards the \nmid-MO Valley. This system will bring our next chance for \nprecipitation, and perhaps the first snow flakes of the season for \nsome. Weak moisture transport over an already cool, dry air mass \nwill limit overall QPF to a few hundredths of an inch at most. \nLatest GEFS and EPS/EPS-AIFS brings a 40-60% probability of at least \n0.01 inch of QPF north of I-80. Additionally, model soundings reveal \nan increasingly dry sub-cloud layer as your progress south across \nthe CWA. With that in mind, PoPs currently peak at 15% Wednesday \nmorning into the early afternoon across northeast Nebraska, \ndecreasing southward. With high temperatures only peaking in the \nupper 30s and low 40s on Wednesday, flurries with a brief rain/snow \nmix and transition to sprinkles is expected generally across \nnortheast NE. Can't fully rule out precipitation chances extending \nfurther south in subsequent forecast packages, though it will depend \non if dynamics can overcome the dry low-levels. A few slick spots \ncan't be ruled out, though widespread travel impacts are not \nexpected. \n\nCAA behind the departing disturbance will keep high temperatures in \nthe low to mid 40s on Thursday, though it is shaping up to be a calm \nand mostly sunny holiday with surface high pressure overspreading \nthe area. \n\nForecast confidence decreases Friday into the weekend. Long-range \nguidance continues to show a brief period of mid-level riding, and \ntherefore a brief warm up into the upper 40s and low 50s, to end the \nwork week. However, a series of shortwave disturbances remain just \nbehind this feature, with low model agreement on the location and \narrival time of these disturbances. Shall these features stay across \nto our north, expect dry conditions with highs in the 50s through \nthe weekend. If this disturbance takes an accelerated southerly \ntrack, which approximately 1/4th of GEFS an EPS/EPS-AIFS ensemble \nmembers suggest, expected highs confined in the 40s with light \nprecipitation chances. We will continue to monitor trends as the \nsystem approaches.",
